WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.048 --> 00:00:02.381
(whooshing)

00:00:03.484 --> 00:00:05.269
- [Narrator] Draw the line.

00:00:05.269 --> 00:00:06.907
People make mistakes all the time.

00:00:06.907 --> 00:00:09.013
They start companies they
think they can manage.

00:00:09.013 --> 00:00:10.764
They have grand and bold visions

00:00:10.764 --> 00:00:12.679
that were a little too grandiose.

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:14.304
This is all perfectly fine.

00:00:14.304 --> 00:00:16.144
It's what being an
entrepreneur or a creative

00:00:16.144 --> 00:00:18.946
or even a business executive is all about.

00:00:18.946 --> 00:00:19.988
We take risks.

00:00:19.988 --> 00:00:21.500
We mess up.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.605
The problem is that when
we get our identity tied up

00:00:24.605 --> 00:00:27.987
in our work, we worry
that any kind of failure

00:00:27.987 --> 00:00:31.755
will then say something
bad about us as a person.

00:00:31.755 --> 00:00:34.292
It's a fear of taking responsibility,

00:00:34.292 --> 00:00:36.732
of admitting that we might have messed up.

00:00:36.732 --> 00:00:38.855
It's the Sunk Cost Fallacy,

00:00:38.855 --> 00:00:42.295
and so we throw good money
and good life after bad

00:00:42.295 --> 00:00:46.669
and end up making
everything so much worse.

00:00:46.669 --> 00:00:48.633
Ego kills what we love.

00:00:48.633 --> 00:00:51.283
Sometimes it comes
close to killing us too.

00:00:51.283 --> 00:00:54.585
"Act with fortitude and honor,"
wrote Alexander Hamilton,

00:00:54.585 --> 00:00:56.829
to a distraught friend
in serious financial

00:00:56.829 --> 00:00:59.239
and legal trouble of his own making.

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:03.187
If you cannot reasonably hope
for a favorable extrication,

00:01:03.187 --> 00:01:05.115
do not plunge deeper.

00:01:05.115 --> 00:01:07.518
Have the courage to make a full stop.

00:01:07.518 --> 00:01:10.580
History are full of people who
suffered abject humiliations,

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:13.312
yet recovered to have long
and impressive careers.

00:01:13.312 --> 00:01:16.195
Politicians who lost
elections or lost offices

00:01:16.195 --> 00:01:19.006
due to indiscretions,
but came back to lead

00:01:19.006 --> 00:01:20.599
after time had passed.

00:01:20.599 --> 00:01:24.646
Actors whose movies bombed,
authors who got writers' block,

00:01:24.646 --> 00:01:27.974
celebrities who made gaffes,
parents who made mistakes,

00:01:27.974 --> 00:01:30.369
entrepreneurs with faltering companies,

00:01:30.369 --> 00:01:34.021
executives who got fired,
athletes who were cut,

00:01:34.021 --> 00:01:36.609
people who lived too well
at the top of the market.

00:01:36.609 --> 00:01:39.633
All of these folks felt
a hard edge of failure

00:01:39.633 --> 00:01:41.042
just like we have.

00:01:41.042 --> 00:01:43.219
When we lose, we have a choice.

00:01:43.219 --> 00:01:46.024
Are you going to make
this a lose-lose situation

00:01:46.024 --> 00:01:48.132
for ourselves and everyone involved?

00:01:48.132 --> 00:01:51.312
Or will there be a lose, and then a win?

00:01:51.312 --> 00:01:53.473
Because you will lose in life.

00:01:53.473 --> 00:01:54.374
It's a fact.

00:01:54.374 --> 00:01:57.051
A doctor has to call a time
of death at some point.

00:01:57.051 --> 00:01:58.145
They just do.

00:01:58.145 --> 00:02:00.207
At any given time in the circle of life,

00:02:00.207 --> 00:02:02.831
we may be aspiring,
succeeding, or failing.

00:02:02.831 --> 00:02:05.431
With wisdom, we understand
that these positions

00:02:05.431 --> 00:02:08.875
are transitory, not
statements about your value

00:02:08.875 --> 00:02:11.033
as a human being.

00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:13.244
When success begins to
slip from your fingers

00:02:13.244 --> 00:02:16.735
for whatever reason, the
response isn't to grip

00:02:16.735 --> 00:02:19.341
and claw so hard that you break things.

00:02:19.341 --> 00:02:22.241
It's to understand that
you must work yourself back

00:02:22.241 --> 00:02:24.207
to the aspirational phase.

00:02:24.207 --> 00:02:27.462
You must get back to first
principles and best practices.

00:02:27.462 --> 00:02:30.482
"He who fears death will
never do anything worthy

00:02:30.482 --> 00:02:33.100
"of a living man," Seneca once said.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.754
You must be strong enough
to maintain your dignity

00:02:35.754 --> 00:02:38.919
and self-respect, because
the only real failure

00:02:38.919 --> 00:02:41.268
is in abandoning your principles.

00:02:41.268 --> 00:02:43.261
Killing what you love
because you can't bear

00:02:43.261 --> 00:02:45.823
to part from it is selfish and stupid.

00:02:45.823 --> 00:02:48.211
If your reputation can't
absorb a few blows,

00:02:48.211 --> 00:02:50.720
it wasn't worth anything
in the first place.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.118
Failure sucks.

00:02:52.118 --> 00:02:55.493
Deal with it with
dignity and self-respect.

00:02:55.493 --> 00:02:59.300
"It can ruin your life only
if it ruins your character."

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.295
Marcus Aurelius.

00:03:01.295 --> 00:03:02.495
Draw the line.

00:03:02.495 --> 00:03:04.912
(calm music)

